---
// Shared "common protocol sections" so every generated protocol covers the
// HARPER / TARGET reporting items the Protocol Checker judges — a structured
// abstract, study size, data-source provenance, data management & QC, planned
// analytical outputs, limitations, human-subjects & governance, registration &
// amendments, adverse-event handling, and funding/COI. Include once per builder
// page (it exposes window.PC), then in buildMarkdown()/buildDocx() splice in:
//   md:   PC.abstractMd(pc) after the title/subtitle, PC.tailMd(pc) before "## References"
//   docx: ...PC.abstractDocx(D, pc) after the subtitle, ...PC.tailDocx(D, pc) before the References heading
//
// pc = {
//   design,            // e.g. "active-comparator new-user cohort"
//   tte,               // true for target-trial-emulation designs (ACNU/CCW/ST)
//   population, exposure, comparator, outcome, data, analysis,
//   groupBy,           // label for Table 1 columns (default "exposure group")
//   dataMeta, studySize, ethics, registration,  // optional; sensible defaults
//   limitations,       // optional design-specific sentence, appended to the generic one
//   plannedOutputs,    // optional array of extra shell tables/figures for this design.
//                      // An entry may be a plain string, or { key, text } where key is
//                      // one of "flow" / "table1" / "results" — that bullet then
//                      // REPLACES the shared one and renders in its position.
//   skipOutputs,       // optional subset of ["flow","table1","results"] — shared
//                      // planned-output bullets this design cannot produce at all
//                      // (only ITS). To print a better version of one, fill its slot
//                      // via plannedOutputs instead of skipping it. An unrecognised
//                      // key, or a non-list, prints a visible defect notice rather
//                      // than silently doing nothing.
//   amendments,        // optional; the raw "amendments" field (see below)
// }
//
// There is deliberately no `version` here. There was, until the twenty-fifth
// run deleted its only consumer — a parenthetical that printed "This is the
// original version of the protocol (v2.0 · 2026-08-01); no amendments have been
// made" — and the key survived the consumer for four runs, documented as part
// of this contract and copied into two builders' pcOf. Nothing in this file has
// read it since. Passing it suggested that the component reconciles a builder's
// version box against the log's Version-number column, and it does not: each
// builder prints its own version in its own document header, and the log is
// whatever the user typed.
//
// ── HARPER item 3 (amendments and updates) — integrating a builder ──────────
//
// The amendments log is emitted by abstractMd()/abstractDocx(), which every
// builder already calls, so ITEM 3 IS SATISFIED WITH NO BUILDER CHANGE AT ALL:
// with no amendment data the protocol states, in its own front matter, that it
// is the original version and no amendments have been made. That is the correct
// claim for a first draft, not a placeholder.
//
// To let the user actually record amendments, a builder adds three lines:
//
//   1. Mount the editor into the form (either side of restore is fine —
//      PC.restore() creates the field itself and repaints afterwards):
//        window.PC && PC.mountAmendments(form);
//      It inserts its own <details> block just above the download buttons and
//      owns a hidden field named "amendments". Nothing else to add to the
//      markup: readForm(), localStorage and ?seed= pick the field up already
//      because it is a normal named form control.
//
//   2. Pass it through pcOf() so the export uses the state the builder read,
//      not the live DOM:
//        amendments: s.amendments,
//      (Omitting this still works — PC falls back to reading the field off the
//      page — but passing it is what keeps export and state in step.)
//
//   3. If the builder has a "Clear all"/reset that writes fields directly:
//        window.PC && PC.syncAmendments(form);
//      after resetting, so the rows repaint. This step is REQUIRED, not
//      optional. Every builder's reset assigns `.value` in script, which fires
//      no change event, and `render()` fires none either — so the editor's
//      "self-heal on the form's change event" never runs on this path, and the
//      panel goes on showing rows the reset has already deleted. Without this
//      call the panel and the document disagree until the user touches the
//      panel again. The editor no longer writes a stale model back (see
//      `stale()` in mountAmendments), so nothing is fabricated — but what a
//      user meets is not a merely out-of-date panel: their first keystroke is
//      DISCARDED and the box repaints from the field under the caret, with no
//      message. That is the right outcome and a poor experience, and it is
//      what forgetting this call actually costs.
//
// Storage format: one line per amendment in the single field "amendments",
// pipe-delimited in HARPER's own column order —
//   version date | version number | section of protocol | amendment | reason
// A cell may contain a "|" if it is written "\|"; a "\" is written "\\".
// A line must hold exactly FIVE such values. Anything else — a bare pipe inside
// a cell, a missing column, a Markdown-style leading pipe, a wrapped line — is
// ambiguous, and a line the parser cannot read in five columns is reproduced
// verbatim in the exported protocol under a heading saying so, rather than being
// split into columns that may not be the ones intended. Anything writing this
// field from outside the editor (a ?seed= link, a hand-off payload) should emit
// five escaped values per line, or expect a visible refusal.
---

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    const nz = (v, d) => (v && String(v).trim() ? String(v).trim() : d);
    const P = (s) => nz(s.population, "(population)");
    const E = (s) => nz(s.exposure, "(exposure/intervention)");
    const C = (s) => nz(s.comparator, "");
    const O = (s) => nz(s.outcome, "(outcome)");
    const contrast = (s) => E(s) + (C(s) ? " versus " + C(s) : "");

    const designLine = (s) => s.tte
      ? nz(s.design, "target-trial-emulation study") + " that emulates a target trial using observational data from " + nz(s.data, "(data source)")
      : nz(s.design, "observational study") + " using data from " + nz(s.data, "(data source)");

    const abstractLines = (s) => s.descriptive ? ([
      ["Objective. ", "To describe and quantify " + O(s) + " among " + P(s) + "."],
      ["Design. ", designLine(s) + "."],
      ["Data source(s). ", nz(s.data, "(data source)") + (s.dataMeta ? " — " + s.dataMeta : "") + "."],
      ["Measures. ", O(s) + ", overall and by the pre-specified strata."],
      ["Analysis. ", nz(s.analysis, "descriptive estimates with 95% confidence intervals") + "."],
      ["Reporting. ", "This protocol follows the HARPER template."],
    ]) : ([
      ["Objective. ", "To estimate the effect of " + contrast(s) + " on " + O(s) + " among " + P(s) + "."],
      ["Design. ", designLine(s) + "."],
      ["Data source(s). ", nz(s.data, "(data source)") + (s.dataMeta ? " — " + s.dataMeta : "") + "."],
      ["Population, exposure, comparator, outcome. ", P(s) + "; " + contrast(s) + "; " + O(s) + "."],
      ["Analysis. ", nz(s.analysis, "the pre-specified primary analysis with 95% confidence intervals") + "."],
      ["Reporting. ", "This protocol follows the HARPER template" + (s.tte ? "; target-trial-emulation elements follow the TARGET statement" : "") + "."],
    ]);

    // `abstractLines` above already branches on s.descriptive; these three did
    // not, so a descriptive protocol exported "Primary-results table — the
    // effect estimate", "Residual and unmeasured confounding cannot be
    // excluded" and a power calculation "to detect the smallest clinically
    // important effect" — three statements about an effect that the very next
    // sentence says is not being estimated. Only the descriptive-analysis
    // builder sets s.descriptive, so these branches reach exactly that one page.
    //
    // The three shared bullets are also KEYED, because `base.concat(own)` with
    // no de-duplication printed each of them twice in eight of the nine
    // builders: every builder's own `plannedOutputs` is a design-specific
    // rewrite of the same three items. A protocol commissioned two flow
    // diagrams, two tables both called Table 1, and two results tables — and on
    // the designs that are not person-level cohorts the SHARED half was the
    // wrong half. There are two different things a builder needs to say about
    // one of these bullets, and they are not the same statement:
    //
    //   "I print this better myself" — give the bullet the slot it fills:
    //       plannedOutputs: [{ key: "flow", text: "Case-selection flow …" }, …]
    //     It then renders IN THAT SLOT'S POSITION. Saying it this way instead
    //     of skipping the slot and appending the replacement is what keeps the
    //     order right: the first version of this dropped `flow` and pushed the
    //     replacement into the trailing block, so SCCS and case-crossover
    //     tabulated the baseline characteristics of a population one bullet
    //     BEFORE saying how that population was ascertained — the same species
    //     of inversion the results-last rule below exists to prevent.
    //     It also means a bullet's identity is written in exactly one place,
    //     so the replacement and the thing it replaces cannot drift apart.
    //
    //   "this design cannot produce this at all" — genuine deletion:
    //       skipOutputs: ["flow", "table1", "results"]
    //     Only interrupted-time-series needs this: its unit of analysis is a
    //     calendar period, so there is no cohort to draw and no baseline to
    //     tabulate. Everywhere else the honest statement is a replacement.
    //
    // Keys, not indices, so this list can be reordered without silently
    // changing which bullet a builder omits or fills.
    const OUTPUT_KEYS = ["flow", "table1", "results"];
    const baseOutputs = (s) => (s.descriptive ? {
      flow: "Population-selection flow diagram (source population → analytic cohort, with exclusions and counts).",
      table1: "Table 1 — characteristics of the study population by " + nz(s.groupBy, "stratum") + ".",
      results: "Primary-results table — each reported rate or proportion with its 95% confidence interval, by site and by stratum.",
    } : {
      flow: "Participant-flow diagram (source population → analytic cohort, with exclusions and counts).",
      table1: "Table 1 — baseline characteristics by " + nz(s.groupBy, "exposure group") + ".",
      results: "Primary-results table — the effect estimate with its 95% confidence interval.",
    });

    const defaultOutputs = (s) => {
      const base = baseOutputs(s);
      const KEYLIST = OUTPUT_KEYS.join(", ");

      // Anything a builder said about these bullets that this component could
      // not act on. Every entry is a full clause, because the failures differ:
      // a misspelled key omits nothing, a bad slot name fills nothing, and a
      // `skipOutputs` that is not a list does neither. Each of them would
      // otherwise leave the duplicate standing with no trace but a string
      // nobody reads — `"table-1"` for `"table1"` is one keystroke, and
      // `skipOutputs: "flow"` without the brackets is what you write when you
      // are dropping exactly one. Reported in the document, below.
      const bad = [];

      const rawDrop = s.skipOutputs;
      const drop = Array.isArray(rawDrop) ? rawDrop.map(String) : [];
      if (rawDrop != null && !Array.isArray(rawDrop)) {
        bad.push("skipOutputs was given as " + JSON.stringify(rawDrop) + " rather than a list, so nothing was omitted");
      }
      drop.forEach(function (k) {
        if (OUTPUT_KEYS.indexOf(k) === -1) {
          bad.push('skipOutputs asked to omit "' + k + '", which is not one of ' + KEYLIST + ', so nothing was omitted for it');
        }
      });

      // A plannedOutputs entry is either a plain string (an extra bullet) or
      // { key, text } claiming one of the shared slots.
      const slot = {}, own = [];
      (Array.isArray(s.plannedOutputs) ? s.plannedOutputs : []).forEach(function (e) {
        if (e && typeof e === "object" && !Array.isArray(e)) {
          const text = String(e.text == null ? "" : e.text).trim();
          if (!text) return;
          if (OUTPUT_KEYS.indexOf(e.key) === -1) {
            bad.push('a planned output claimed the slot "' + String(e.key) + '", which is not one of ' + KEYLIST + ', so it was added as an extra bullet instead');
            own.push(text);
            return;
          }
          slot[e.key] = text;
          return;
        }
        if (e && String(e).trim()) own.push(String(e).trim());
      });

      // Order: flow, Table 1, the design's own outputs, then the results table.
      // A plain `shared.concat(own)` reads correctly only while all three
      // shared bullets are present — drop the middle one and the results table
      // floats up to sit above the Table 1 that has not been commissioned yet.
      // A protocol's outputs list is read in the order the study produces
      // them, so the results shell goes last whatever else is dropped.
      const line = (k) => (drop.indexOf(k) !== -1 ? null : (slot[k] || base[k]));
      const out = [];
      ["flow", "table1"].forEach(function (k) { const v = line(k); if (v) out.push(v); });
      own.forEach(function (v) { out.push(v); });
      const res = line("results");
      if (res) out.push(res);

      // Never hand back an empty list: `tailMd` would print the heading with
      // nothing under it, and a protocol whose planned-outputs section is a
      // bare heading reads as finished while it commissions nothing at all.
      // This runs BEFORE the defect notice below, because the two can happen
      // together — a builder that drops every slot AND misspells a key — and
      // in that case the sentence telling the researcher the section is
      // unfinished is the one that must not be crowded out by the bug report.
      if (!out.length) {
        out.push("(No analytical outputs are specified — this protocol does not yet say what tables or figures it will produce. This section has to be completed before the protocol is circulated.)");
      }

      if (bad.length) {
        out.push("⚠ PROTOCOL-GENERATOR DEFECT — " + bad.join("; ") +
          ". This list may therefore repeat an item or leave one out. Please report this — it is a bug in the tool, not a decision about your study.");
      }
      return out;
    };

    const limitationsText = (s) =>
      (s.descriptive
        ? "No causal contrast is estimated and none should be read into these numbers; the outcome and exposure may be misclassified in secondary data, and generalisability is bounded by the source populations of the contributing data sources."
        : "Residual and unmeasured confounding cannot be excluded; the outcome and exposure may be misclassified in secondary data; and generalisability is bounded by the source population.") +
      (s.limitations ? " " + s.limitations : "");

    const ethicsText = (s) => nz(s.ethics,
      "Each participating data source obtains local institutional review board / ethics approval (or a documented waiver) before data extraction; only de-identified data are analysed.");

    /* ── HARPER item 3: amendments and updates ─────────────────────────────
       Every builder failed this item, because a protocol that has never been
       amended still has to SAY so — "no log" and "no changes yet" look the
       same in an exported document, and a reader cannot tell which they are
       looking at. Built once here rather than nine times, so the nine builders
       cannot drift into nine incompatible amendment tables.

       Column order follows the HARPER template's own table
       (drugepi.gitlab-pages.partners.org/harper): version date, version
       number, section of protocol, amendment or update, reason.

       Storage is one line per amendment in a single form field named
       "amendments", pipe-delimited in that same order — the idiom the builders
       already use for their period strips and look-back windows, so their
       existing readForm / localStorage / ?seed= paths carry it with no change.
       A pipe TYPED INTO THE EDITOR survives, because it is escaped on the way
       into the field and unescaped on the way out. Only the trailing Reason
       column used to be protected, so a pipe in one of the first four — "7 | 8"
       in Section of protocol, meaning sections 7 and 8, is the obvious one —
       shifted every later cell one column left. The exported HARPER amendment
       table then read "Section: 7", "Amendment: 8", and the description of what
       changed appeared under Reason: a complete, plausible, wrong row.

       That escaping covers exactly one of the four ways text reaches this
       field. A `?seed=` link, a saved draft and a `?seed=paste` payload all
       write it raw, and the editor that does the escaping is mounted on two of
       nine builders. So the wrong row above was still reachable, verbatim, on
       every builder — and the parser cannot repair it, because nothing in the
       stored text distinguishes "a cell containing a pipe" from "an extra
       column". A line the parser cannot read in five columns is therefore
       refused rather than split: see amLog. */
    const AM_FIELD = "amendments";
    const AM_COLS = ["Version date", "Version number", "Section of protocol", "Amendment or update", "Reason"];

    /* Every Markdown table cell on this site goes through here.
       This is the FOURTH copy of this escaper: `case-crossover`, `sccs` and
       `descriptive-analysis` each grew their own after a pipe in a code set
       ("ATC M01A* | excluding M01AX") emitted a three-cell row against a
       two-column header, and all three stopped at the pipe. Escaping `|`
       without escaping `\` is not a smaller version of the fix, it is a
       different bug: a cell holding the two characters `\|` is emitted as
       `\\|`, which GFM reads as an escaped BACKSLASH followed by a live
       delimiter. Parsed with micromark's real table extension,
       "ATC N05A* \| excluding N05AN01" renders as "ATC N05A* \" — the second
       half of the code set is gone, the row still has the header's cell count,
       and the .docx (which needs no escaping) keeps the whole string. Two
       exported documents then specify different exposures for the same study.
       `\|` is not exotic for this audience: it is R's regex alternation
       (grepl("N05AH\\|N05AX", code)) and it is what a Markdown-literate author
       types to mean a literal pipe.
       Newlines end a table row outright, so they collapse to a space here for
       the same reason — and that means a LONE CARRIAGE RETURN too. GFM counts
       CR, LF and CRLF as line endings, so the obvious `/\r?\n/` handles two of
       the three: a cell holding "Section 6\rand 7" kept its CR, ended the row
       there, and the remainder became an unnumbered row with every value shifted
       one column left, while the .docx printed the cell whole. That is the same
       defect this function exists to prevent, one character further along. */
    function mdCell(v) {
      return String(v == null ? "" : v).replace(/([\\|])/g, "\\$1").replace(/\r\n?|\n/g, " ");
    }

    // Escape backslash first, so unescaping is unambiguous.
    var amEsc = function (c) { return String(c == null ? "" : c).replace(/\\/g, "\\\\").replace(/\|/g, "\\|"); };
    var amUnesc = function (c) { return String(c).replace(/\\([\\|])/g, "$1"); };
    // Split on pipes that are not escaped. Drafts written before this change
    // contain bare pipes and still split on them, which is the behaviour they
    // already had; anything typed from now on round-trips intact.
    function amSegments(line) {
      var out = [], cur = "", i = 0;
      while (i < line.length) {
        var ch = line.charAt(i);
        if (ch === "\\" && i + 1 < line.length) { cur += ch + line.charAt(i + 1); i += 2; continue; }
        if (ch === "|") { out.push(cur); cur = ""; i += 1; continue; }
        cur += ch; i += 1;
      }
      out.push(cur);
      return out;
    }
    function amSplit(line) {
      var out = amSegments(line);
      // Five columns; anything beyond the fifth belongs to Reason. Only called
      // on lines that HAVE five segments — see amLog — so neither the padding
      // below nor the overflow join fires on anything the tool prints.
      // A lone CR inside a cell survives amLog's /\r?\n/ split and then ends the
      // Markdown row, so it is flattened here rather than in one emitter — the
      // Word file has to see the same cell the Markdown does.
      var flat = function (c) { return amUnesc(c).replace(/[\r\n]+/g, " ").trim(); };
      var cells = [];
      for (var j = 0; j < 4; j++) cells.push(flat(out[j] === undefined ? "" : out[j]));
      cells.push(flat(out.slice(4).join("|")));
      return cells;
    }

    // Read a builder field straight off the page. Used only as a fallback when
    // a builder has not yet been updated to pass the value through pcOf(), so
    // that shipping this component fixes the item everywhere at once instead of
    // waiting on nine separate edits. Export always runs from the live form, so
    // this reads the same values the builder itself would hand over.
    function fromForm(name) {
      try {
        var f = document.querySelector("[data-builder]") || document.querySelector("form");
        if (!f) return "";
        var el = f.querySelector('[name="' + name + '"]');
        return el && el.value != null ? String(el.value) : "";
      } catch (e) {
        console.warn("[PC] could not read field", name, e);
        return "";
      }
    }

    /* Which stored lines this tool is entitled to print as a five-column row.

       `writeRows` joins exactly five cells with " | ", each through `amEsc`, so
       EVERY line the editor writes has exactly five unescaped-pipe segments —
       blank cells included, because a blank cell still contributes its
       separators. A line with any other segment count therefore did not come
       from THIS editor. Almost always it arrived raw, from a `?seed=` link, a
       saved draft or a pasted payload, none of which is escaped: `amEsc` is
       called in exactly one place, inside the editor, which two of nine builders
       mount. The one other source is a draft written before the escaping landed
       (2026-08-23), which stored bare pipes — and such a line is ambiguous in
       exactly the same way, so refusing it is right for the same reason.

       For such a line the tool cannot know which value belongs in which column,
       and until now it guessed. Six segments is the case the block comment above
       claims to have fixed and had not: "… | 7 | 8 | Sections 7 and 8 harmonised
       | Reviewer comment", where the sender meant Section = "7 | 8", exported as
       Section 7, Amendment 8, with the description of the change filed under
       Reason — a complete, plausible, wrong row that the Markdown and the Word
       file agreed on. Fewer than five is the same defect pointed the other way:
       omit the version number and every later value shifts one column left, and
       a newline inside a cell splits one amendment into fragments of three and
       one segments, the continuation prose landing under Version date. A
       Markdown-style leading pipe shifts the whole row right and reports the
       Version date as blank.

       So parse the lines it can, reproduce the rest verbatim, and say which is
       which. The one thing this cannot see is a five-segment RAW line — a sender
       who wrote five values, one of them containing a pipe, is byte-identical to
       an editor row. Nothing here validates a row's content, and no wording
       below claims it does.

       One thing this deliberately gives up, because it was a real design and not
       an accident. The overflow join in amSplit protected the trailing column:
       a six-segment raw line whose sixth value belonged to Reason produced a
       CORRECT row, and "… | Washout lengthened | Reviewer A | B disagreed" is
       now refused instead. That line is genuinely indistinguishable from the
       "7 | 8" case — same shape, pipe in a different column — so protecting one
       means guessing on the other, and the sender sees their line in full either
       way. The trade is one correct row lost for a class of confident wrong rows
       refused.

       Numbering is by position in the whole log, so a refused entry keeps its
       place: the table shows rows 1 and 3 and the refusal block names entry 2.
       Renumbering the survivors 1, 2 would destroy the only ordering information
       a refused entry has left, and the numbers in an amendment table are how
       amendments get cited. */
    function amLog(s) {
      var raw = (s && s.amendments != null) ? s.amendments : fromForm(AM_FIELD);
      var out = [];
      String(raw == null ? "" : raw).split(/\r?\n/).forEach(function (line) {
        if (!line.trim()) return;
        if (amSegments(line).length === AM_COLS.length) {
          var cells = amSplit(line);
          // A row of nothing but separators is not an entry, and never was.
          if (!cells.some(function (c) { return c; })) return;
          out.push({ ok: true, cells: cells, line: line, values: AM_COLS.length });
        } else {
          out.push({ ok: false, cells: null, line: line, values: amSegments(line).length });
        }
      });
      out.forEach(function (e, i) { e.no = i + 1; });
      return out;
    }
    function amendmentRows(s) {
      return amLog(s).filter(function (e) { return e.ok; }).map(function (e) { return e.cells; });
    }

    /* Two claims live here and only one of them is the tool's to make.

       The EMPTY case is a statement about this document — a log is kept here
       and it is empty — and it stays exactly as it was, for the reason at the
       top of this block: without it, "no log kept" and "no changes yet" are
       indistinguishable in a finished protocol.

       The NON-EMPTY case used to assert a history: "has been amended 3 times
       since its original version". The tool cannot count amendments; it can
       count rows, and the two come apart constantly. Recording the original
       version as row 1 is ordinary practice and made the prose say "amended
       1 time" about a table whose only row was the original version. A
       hand-off payload with a newline inside its Reason cell splits into three
       rows, so one amendment was reported as three, with the continuation text
       sitting in the Version date column. The sentence now describes the table
       standing beneath it, which is the only thing it can actually see — and
       it names the columns rather than promising every row has filled them,
       because a row needs only one non-empty cell to be exported.

       The version string used to be appended to BOTH sentences as a
       parenthetical. On the four builders that have a Version box that printed
       "This is the original version of the protocol (v2.0 · 2026-08-01); no
       amendments have been made." — a sentence carrying its own refutation, on
       a stock page load with no link and no draft involved, and on two of those
       four (ACNU and descriptive-analysis) there is no amendments editor with
       which to make it true. It was also pure duplication: every builder with a
       version field already prints it in the document's own header block.

       The clause "; a cell the author left blank is shown as an em dash" is
       gone, and it is the cheapest correct change in this whole area. One glyph
       carried three states: the author left the cell blank, the parser
       manufactured the cell by padding a short line, and the author typed an em
       dash — ordinarily meaning "not applicable". The sentence asserted the
       first of the three, unconditionally, and the third is byte-identical to
       the first in storage, so no parser work can rescue it. Refusing short
       lines (see amLog) removes the second state; deleting eleven words removes
       a false claim about a person from the remaining two. No legend replaces
       it, because every legend anyone proposed is false in the third state too. */
    var amPlural = function (n, one, many) { return n + " " + (n === 1 ? one : many); };
    function amendmentIntro(s, log) {
      var ok = log.filter(function (e) { return e.ok; }).length;
      var bad = log.length - ok;
      var cols = "the date of the change, the version it produced, the section of the protocol " +
        "affected, what changed, and the reason for the change";
      if (!log.length) {
        return "This is the original version of the protocol; no amendments have been made. " +
          "Any later change is recorded here as a numbered amendment with its date, the version it produced, " +
          "the section of the protocol affected, what changed, and the reason for the change.";
      }
      /* Zero readable rows and some refused ones must NOT fall through to the
         empty branch. That branch says "no amendments have been made", which
         would convert a log the tool could not read into an affirmative denial
         that any amendment exists — strictly worse than the bug being fixed. */
      if (!ok) {
        return "This protocol's amendment log is not empty, but " +
          (bad === 1 ? "its one entry is not" : "none of its " + bad + " entries is") +
          " in the five-column form this table requires, so no table is shown. " +
          (bad === 1 ? "It is" : "They are") + " reproduced below exactly as stored, because splitting " +
          (bad === 1 ? "it" : "them") + " into columns would have produced a specific date, version and section " +
          "that may not be the ones intended. What this protocol's amendments were cannot be established " +
          "from the entries as they stand.";
      }
      if (!bad) {
        return "The amendment table below records " + amPlural(ok, "entry", "entries") +
          ". Its columns are " + cols + ".";
      }
      return "The amendment table below has a numbered row for each of the " + log.length +
        " entries in this protocol's amendment log. Its columns are " + cols + ". " +
        amPlural(bad, "entry", "entries") + " did not arrive in that five-column form: " +
        (bad === 1 ? "its row carries a placeholder" : "their rows carry a placeholder") +
        " instead of columns, and the " + (bad === 1 ? "entry is" : "entries are") +
        " reproduced beneath the table exactly as stored, because splitting " +
        (bad === 1 ? "it" : "them") + " into columns would have produced a specific date, version and " +
        "section that may not be the ones intended.";
    }

    /* The lead for the refusal block, shared by both exports so they cannot
       drift. It names the three things that actually cause a refusal, because a
       refusal the sender can act on is not a guess — and the Markdown-table
       leading pipe is the likeliest of the three by some distance. */
    var AM_REFUSE_HEAD = "Entries that could not be read as five columns";
    /* One sentence, both exports, so they cannot drift — which is also why the
       advice is spelled out in words. Writing the escape as \| would be correct
       in the stored field and wrong on the page: a Markdown reader renders \|
       as a bare pipe, so the instruction would print as "write it as |". */
    var AM_REFUSE_LEAD = "Each line below is reproduced exactly as it is stored. " +
      "If an entry was copied from a Markdown table, remove the \"|\" at the start and end of the line; " +
      "if a cell is meant to contain a \"|\", put a backslash in front of it; " +
      "if an entry runs over more than one line, put it on one line.";
    /* "…where this table has 5 columns" sent a sender counting columns to
       repair their line to a table with SIX headed cells, because both emitters
       prepend a No. So count what the sender actually controls — the values on
       the line — and say nothing about the table's shape. */
    var amRefuseLabel = function (e) {
      return "Entry " + e.no + " — " + amPlural(e.values, "value", "values") +
        ", where an entry needs exactly " + AM_COLS.length + ":";
    };
    /* A refused entry still occupies a numbered row in the table, and this is
       not decoration. Amendment tables get lifted out of the protocol — into a
       submission package, a change-control form, a reviewer's summary — and a
       block printed beneath the table does not travel with it. Without a stub
       row a three-entry log with one refusal becomes a two-entry table the
       moment anyone selects it, and a missing amendment is not obviously safer
       than a mis-columned one. The stub asserts nothing about any column: the
       four cells after the notice are genuinely empty, not em dashes. */
    var amStubNotice = function (e) {
      return "Entry " + e.no + " could not be read as five columns; it is reproduced in full beneath this table";
    };
    var amStubCells = function (e) { return [amStubNotice(e), "", "", "", ""]; };

    /* A refused line is reproduced VERBATIM, so it cannot go in a code span:
       one backtick in the sender's text would end the span and the rest would
       be read as Markdown. A fence longer than the longest backtick run in the
       content is the only construct that holds arbitrary text. */
    function mdFence(text) {
      var longest = 0;
      String(text).replace(/`+/g, function (m) { longest = Math.max(longest, m.length); return m; });
      var f = new Array(Math.max(3, longest + 1) + 1).join("`");
      return f + "\n" + text + "\n" + f;
    }

    function amendmentsMd(s) {
      var log = amLog(s);
      var out = "## Amendments and updates\n\n" + amendmentIntro(s, log);
      if (!log.length) return out + "\n";
      // mdCell escapes the backslash as well as the pipe — see its comment for
      // why the pipe alone is not a smaller version of the fix.
      var esc = function (c) { return mdCell(c || "—"); };
      // ONE pass over ONE ordered list. Two loops — readable rows, then the
      // refused lines appended — would silently re-order the log and renumber
      // the survivors, which is a new wrong-document defect in the shape of the
      // one being fixed. `e.no` is the position in the log, so the table shows
      // gaps where an entry was refused.
      if (log.some(function (e) { return e.ok; })) {
        out += "\n\n| No. | " + AM_COLS.join(" | ") + " |\n|---|" + AM_COLS.map(function () { return "---"; }).join("|") + "|\n";
        log.forEach(function (e) {
          var cells = e.ok ? e.cells.map(esc) : amStubCells(e).map(mdCell);
          out += "| " + e.no + " | " + cells.join(" | ") + " |\n";
        });
      }
      var bad = log.filter(function (e) { return !e.ok; });
      if (bad.length) {
        // A blank line, not one newline. With no readable entry there is no
        // table above, so `out` ends mid-paragraph and a single newline is a
        // soft break: the refusal heading rendered INSIDE the intro sentence,
        // while the .docx emitted it as its own bold paragraph. The all-refused
        // case had been checked as a string and not as a rendered document.
        out += (/\n\n$/.test(out) ? "" : "\n") + "\n**" + AM_REFUSE_HEAD + "**\n\n" + AM_REFUSE_LEAD + "\n";
        bad.forEach(function (e) { out += "\n" + amRefuseLabel(e) + "\n\n" + mdFence(e.line) + "\n"; });
      }
      return out;
    }

    // Keyed tail sections. A builder can drop any it already prints itself via
    // pc.skip (e.g. AsPEN builders skip "studySize"/"ethics"). Each entry is
    // [key, heading, bodyLines[], kind?] where kind "bullets" renders a list.
    function tailSections(s) {
      const skip = s.skip || [];
      return [
        ["studySize", "Study size and feasibility", [nz(s.studySize, s.descriptive
          ? "There is no effect size to detect, so the study is sized for PRECISION rather than power: for every reported rate or proportion the expected number of events and the person-time available are established per data source and per stratum in a pilot extraction, and the expected width of the 95% confidence interval is reported alongside them. Cells that will fall below the small-cell suppression rule are identified before the analysis is executed."
          : "Power is assessed per data source from observed exposure and outcome frequencies in a pilot extraction; the study targets ≥ 80% power to detect the smallest clinically important effect at a two-sided α = 0.05. Feasibility (available person-time and event counts) is confirmed before the analysis is executed.")]],
        ["provenance", "Data sources and provenance", [nz(s.dataMeta, "For each source, we record its original purpose, type (claims / EHR / registry), geographic setting, calendar coverage, any record linkage, and the analytic software used.")]],
        ["dataMgmt", "Data management and quality control", ["Source data are extracted and transformed into the analytic dataset with all derived-variable definitions retained and version-controlled. Analytic code is peer-reviewed; variable distributions, cross-variable logic checks, and expected code frequencies are inspected; and missing data are quantified and handled as pre-specified."]],
        ["outputs", "Planned analytical outputs (shell tables and figures)", defaultOutputs(s), "bullets"],
        ["limitations", "Limitations", [limitationsText(s)]],
        ["ethics", "Human subjects, data governance, and dissemination", [ethicsText(s) + " Findings are disseminated via a peer-reviewed manuscript and conference presentation; data-sharing and code-availability follow each source's governance agreements."]],
        /* The second sentence used to read "Amendments are version-controlled
           and logged with date and rationale." It is eight headings below a
           table this document prints, and it certified two properties of that
           table that the table itself can refute. `amLog` admits a row on ONE
           non-empty cell and both emitters print an empty cell as an em dash,
           so filling in only "Amendment or update" — which is what someone
           recording a change in a hurry does — exported

             | 1 | — | — | — | Washout window widened to 90 days | — |

           under a sentence asserting that the date and the rationale were
           logged. Reproduced in a real .md and a real word/document.xml. In the
           all-refused case it is worse: the front matter says what this
           protocol's amendments were cannot be established from the entries as
           they stand, and this sentence, in the same file, says they were
           logged with their dates and reasons.

           It now points at the table instead of certifying it, which is true in
           every state the log can be in and is also the more useful sentence: a
           reader looking for the amendment history is told where it is. What
           each row does or does not carry is the table's business, and the
           front matter above it no longer promises that every row is full
           either. No legend for the em dash — the twenty-eighth run's argument
           against one still holds, because an author who types "—" meaning
           "not applicable" is indistinguishable, on the page, from a cell left
           blank.

           "version-controlled" went with "logged with date and rationale", one
           clause later than it should have. The first draft of this change kept
           it, and a reviewer ran the same reproduction against the kept clause:
           the same row prints an em dash in Version NUMBER too, and on five of
           the nine builders the document carries no version string anywhere at
           all — only "drafted <today>", where today is the date the file was
           downloaded. A certification the document cannot evidence is a
           certification either way, whichever cell it is about. What is left is
           purely locative, which is the only thing this section can see. */
        ["registration", "Registration and amendments", ["The protocol is registered before analysis (e.g. the EU PAS Register / ClinicalTrials.gov) where applicable" + (s.registration ? ": " + s.registration : "") + ". Amendments are recorded in the amendment table in this protocol's front matter."]],
        ["ae", "Adverse-event reporting", ["As a secondary-data analysis of a pre-specified outcome, individual adverse-event reporting does not apply; safety is addressed through the pre-specified outcome analyses."]],
        ["funding", "Funding and conflicts of interest", ["Funding source(s) and each author's conflicts of interest are declared in the final report."]],
      ].filter((x) => !skip.includes(x[0]));
    }

    /* The field has to exist before anything restores into it. If it were
       created only when the editor mounts — which a builder naturally does
       after restoring — then a saved draft's amendments would land nowhere,
       and the first render would write the now-empty field back over them.
       Silent loss of the user's own text, so restore() creates it too and the
       mount order stops mattering. */
    function ensureAmendmentField(form) {
      var field = form.querySelector('[name="' + AM_FIELD + '"]');
      if (!field) {
        // A hidden <input> looks like the obvious choice and is a trap: for
        // type="hidden" the value IDL attribute reflects the CONTENT attribute,
        // so assigning .value also moves .defaultValue. Every builder's
        // "Clear all" resets fields with `el.value = el.defaultValue`, which on
        // such an input is a no-op — the amendments would survive a reset that
        // cleared everything else. A textarea's defaultValue is its markup text
        // and never moves, so this field resets like all the others.
        field = document.createElement("textarea");
        field.name = AM_FIELD;
        field.hidden = true;
        field.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
        field.tabIndex = -1;
        form.appendChild(field);
      }
      return field;
    }

    /* Mount the amendment editor into a builder's form. Idempotent, so a page
       may call it more than once, before or after restore. The rows are a view
       over one hidden field named "amendments"; the field is the single source
       of truth, so the builder's own readForm / localStorage / ?seed= paths
       need no change. */
    function mountAmendments(form, opts) {
      if (!form) return null;
      opts = opts || {};
      var existing = form.querySelector("[data-pc-amendments]");
      if (existing) { existing.__pcSync(); return existing; }

      var field = ensureAmendmentField(form);

      var box = document.createElement("details");
      box.setAttribute("data-pc-amendments", "");
      box.className = "border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl bg-white shadow-sm";
      box.innerHTML =
        // The badge is part of the summary, not of the body, because the body
        // is behind a <details> that ships CLOSED. A refusal the reader has to
        // expand a collapsed section to discover is not visible on screen, and
        // the whole case for putting this editor on a page is that a recipient
        // holding an unreadable entry can repair it — which they will not do if
        // nothing tells them there is one.
        '<summary class="cursor-pointer select-none px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-slate-900 hover:bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl">' +
        // The space before the badge is load-bearing: without it the summary's
        // text content reads "…HARPER front matter1 entry could not be read" to
        // a screen reader, which the margin class hides from a sighted reader.
        'Amendments and updates <span class="font-normal text-slate-500 text-sm">· HARPER front matter</span> ' +
        '<span data-pc-am-badge></span></summary>' +
        '<div class="px-5 pb-5 space-y-3">' +
        '<p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">A protocol that has never been amended is not deficient — but it has to <em>say</em> so, ' +
        'because "no amendments yet" and "no log kept" look identical in a finished document. Leave this empty for version 1 and the protocol states ' +
        'that it is the original version. Add a row each time you change the protocol after circulating it.</p>' +
        // A CONTAINER, not a media query. What decides whether five inputs and
        // a delete button fit on one line is the width of this panel, and the
        // panel sits in the form column of a two-column layout: at a 1024px
        // viewport that column is 422px wide, and at 900px — where the layout
        // collapses to one column — it is 852px. A `sm:` breakpoint reads the
        // viewport and therefore gets it exactly backwards, turning the wide
        // layout ON at 640px in a 550px column.
        '<div data-pc-am-rows class="space-y-2 @container"></div>' +
        // The notes live outside the rows container so they can be rewritten on
        // every keystroke without rebuilding the inputs, which would take the
        // caret with it.
        '<div data-pc-am-note class="space-y-2"></div>' +
        '<button type="button" data-pc-am-add class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 border border-brand-300 hover:bg-brand-50 px-3 py-1.5 rounded transition">+ Add an amendment</button>' +
        "</div>";

      var anchor = opts.before && form.querySelector(opts.before);
      if (!anchor) { var dl = form.querySelector("[data-action='download-docx']"); anchor = dl ? dl.closest("div") : null; }
      if (anchor && anchor.parentNode === form) form.insertBefore(box, anchor); else form.appendChild(box);

      var rowsEl = box.querySelector("[data-pc-am-rows]");
      var noteEl = box.querySelector("[data-pc-am-note]");
      var badgeEl = box.querySelector("[data-pc-am-badge]");
      var lastWritten = null;
      var INPUT = "min-w-0 border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-xs focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500";

      /* `model` is the panel's own list of rows and is the thing the editor
         edits. It is NOT re-derived from the field on every interaction, and
         that distinction is the whole reason this variable exists: the field
         legitimately drops rows that are still entirely blank, so re-reading it
         made a half-finished row disappear underneath the user. "+ Add an
         amendment" pressed twice before typing anything used to leave one row,
         because the second press re-read a field the first press had written
         nothing into. The field remains the single source of truth for EXPORT;
         the model is the single source of truth for what is on SCREEN, and
         `sync` is the one place the model is rebuilt from the field. */
      /* An entry is either a five-cell ROW the editor may edit, or a RAW line
         the parser refused. Before this distinction existed the model held only
         rows, so `sync` ran the misparse into five boxes and the next
         `writeRows` — any keystroke, any delete, or one click on "+ Add", which
         touches no existing row — wrote that misparse back over the sender's
         text, into the field and into the saved draft. Reproduced: a seeded
         "… | 7 | 8 | Sections 7 and 8 harmonised | Reviewer comment" came back
         after a reload with the pipe between 7 and 8 promoted to a hard column
         boundary. Before the click the sender's intent was still recoverable
         from the draft; after it, it was gone. A raw entry carries its line and
         is re-emitted byte-for-byte, so nothing the tool could not read is
         rewritten by the tool. */
      var model = [];
      var isRaw = function (e) { return e && e.raw !== undefined; };

      var readRows = function () {
        return amLog({ amendments: field.value }).map(function (e) {
          return e.ok ? e.cells : { raw: e.line, values: e.values, no: e.no };
        });
      };

      // Can an <input type="date"> actually display this string? Anything else
      // it silently discards, showing an empty box for a value that exports.
      var dateShowable = function (raw) {
        if (raw === "") return true;
        var probe = document.createElement("input");
        probe.type = "date";
        probe.value = raw;
        return probe.value === raw;
      };
      var writeRows = function (rows) {
        // One map over one ordered list, so a refused line keeps its place among
        // the rows. Drop rows the user left entirely blank, so an accidental
        // "+ Add" never becomes an empty amendment in the exported protocol — a
        // raw entry is never blank and is never dropped.
        var text = rows.filter(function (r) {
          return isRaw(r) || r.some(function (c) { return c.trim(); });
        }).map(function (r) {
          return isRaw(r) ? r.raw
            : r.map(function (c) { return amEsc(String(c).replace(/\r?\n/g, " ").trim()); }).join(" | ");
        }).join("\n");
        field.value = text;
        lastWritten = text;
        form.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", { bubbles: true }));
      };
      /* The model is a VIEW of the field, and the field is what exports. If the
         field changed behind the editor's back the model is stale, and writing
         it back RESURRECTS rows the builder has just deleted. Reproduced in
         Chromium on all seven builders that did not yet mount this editor:
         record an amendment, press "Clear all" — which empties the field and
         leaves the panel untouched, because assigning `.value` in script fires
         no change event and `render()` fires none either — then click "+ Add an
         amendment", which touches no existing row. The cleared amendment was
         back in the field, in the saved draft and in the exported protocol,
         under a sentence counting it as an amendment the investigator made.
         The two builders that call PC.syncAmendments after their reset were
         immune, which is what step 3 of the recipe is for; this guard is what
         makes forgetting step 3 degrade instead of fabricate. Every path that
         writes the model checks first.

         The comparison rests on an invariant kept in three other functions: a
         <textarea>'s value getter normalises line endings, so `t.value = "a\rb"`
         reads back "a\nb". If `writeRows` ever wrote a CR, this would be
         PERMANENTLY true and every keystroke and every ✕ would be dropped for
         the life of the page. It cannot: an <input> cannot hold a CR, writeRows
         collapses /\r?\n/ per cell, and amSplit strips [\r\n]+ from every cell.
         The twenty-eighth run was bitten once by exactly this character. */
      var stale = function () { return field.value !== lastWritten; };
      /* The notes have to be recomputed on every KEYSTROKE, not only on a
         repaint. The first attempt at this ran once per paint, and because
         typing calls writeRows without repainting, the very first amendment
         anyone recorded — add a row, type into it — left "This row is still
         empty…" on screen above a document that already carried the row. That
         is the same screen-disagrees-with-document defect this panel is
         supposed to remove, pointed the other way. */
      var paintNotes = function (rows) {
        noteEl.innerHTML = "";
        var refused = rows.filter(isRaw);
        // Repainted on every keystroke with the rest of the notes, so closing
        // the panel never hides the fact that something in it needs attention.
        if (refused.length) {
          badgeEl.className = "ml-2 align-middle text-xs font-semibold text-amber-900 bg-amber-100 border border-amber-300 rounded px-2 py-0.5";
          badgeEl.textContent = refused.length === 1
            ? "1 entry could not be read"
            : refused.length + " entries could not be read";
        } else {
          badgeEl.className = "";
          badgeEl.textContent = "";
        }
        var editable = rows.filter(function (r) { return !isRaw(r); });
        var willExport = refused.length + editable.filter(function (r) {
          return r.some(function (c) { return String(c == null ? "" : c).trim(); });
        }).length;
        if (refused.length) {
          // Say the same thing the document says, so the panel and the exported
          // protocol cannot disagree about how many entries were understood.
          var ref = document.createElement("p");
          ref.className = "text-xs text-amber-800 bg-amber-50 border border-amber-300 rounded px-3 py-2";
          ref.textContent = (refused.length === 1
            ? "One entry could not be read as five columns, so it is not shown in the boxes above"
            : refused.length + " entries could not be read as five columns, so they are not shown in the boxes above")
            + ". The protocol reproduces " + (refused.length === 1 ? "it" : "them")
            + " exactly as stored, under a heading saying so, rather than guessing which value belongs in which column. "
            + "Retype " + (refused.length === 1 ? "it" : "each of them") + " into a new row and remove the original.";
          noteEl.appendChild(ref);
        }
        if (!willExport) {
          var none = document.createElement("p");
          none.className = "text-xs text-slate-500 italic";
          // This branch is only reached when EVERY row on screen is empty, so
          // the count of rows is the count of empty ones.
          none.textContent = editable.length === 0
            ? "No amendments — the protocol will state that this is the original version."
            : (editable.length === 1 ? "This row is still empty" : "These " + editable.length + " rows are still empty")
              + ", so the protocol states that this is the original version until you fill something in.";
          noteEl.appendChild(none);
        }
        var unshowable = editable.filter(function (r) {
          return !dateShowable(String(r[0] == null ? "" : r[0]));
        }).length;
        if (unshowable) {
          var warn = document.createElement("p");
          warn.className = "text-xs text-amber-800 bg-amber-50 border border-amber-300 rounded px-3 py-2";
          warn.textContent = unshowable === 1
            ? "One Version date is not a calendar date, so it is shown in a plain box instead of the date picker. It is exported exactly as written — replace it with a yyyy-mm-dd date if that is not what you want."
            : unshowable + " Version dates are not calendar dates, so they are shown in plain boxes instead of the date picker. They are exported exactly as written — replace them with yyyy-mm-dd dates if that is not what you want.";
          noteEl.appendChild(warn);
        }
      };

      var paint = function (rows) {
        rowsEl.innerHTML = "";
        rows.forEach(function (r, i) {
          if (isRaw(r)) {
            /* Shown, not editable. Five boxes would be a claim about which value
               belongs in which column, and the refusal exists precisely because
               the tool cannot make that claim. The line is on screen verbatim so
               the user can retype it, and the ✕ removes it once they have. */
            var bad = document.createElement("div");
            bad.className = "rounded border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-3 py-2 flex items-start gap-2";
            var txt = document.createElement("div");
            txt.className = "flex-1 min-w-0";
            var lbl = document.createElement("p");
            lbl.className = "text-xs text-amber-900 font-semibold";
            lbl.textContent = amPlural(r.values, "value", "values") + ", where a row has " + AM_COLS.length +
              " columns — reproduced in the protocol exactly as stored:";
            var pre = document.createElement("pre");
            pre.className = "text-xs font-mono text-amber-900 whitespace-pre-wrap break-words mt-1";
            pre.textContent = r.raw;
            txt.appendChild(lbl); txt.appendChild(pre);
            bad.appendChild(txt);
            var rm = document.createElement("button");
            rm.type = "button";
            rm.className = "text-xs text-slate-400 hover:text-rose-600 px-2 py-1.5";
            rm.textContent = "✕";
            rm.setAttribute("aria-label", "Remove the entry that could not be read, " + (i + 1));
            // A stale model's indices do not address the rows the field now
            // holds, so re-read and repaint rather than delete by position.
            // The click is dropped; the alternative is removing whichever entry
            // happens to sit at index i in a list the user is not looking at.
            rm.addEventListener("click", function () {
              if (stale()) { sync(); return; }
              rows.splice(i, 1); writeRows(rows); paint(rows);
            });
            bad.appendChild(rm);
            rowsEl.appendChild(bad);
            return;
          }
          var row = document.createElement("div");
          /* Every track is a `minmax(0, …)` and every input carries `min-w-0`,
             for the reason globals.css already records about `.protocol-form
             select`: a grid item's default `min-width: auto` is its min-content
             width, so an <input> holds its track open at ~177px whatever the
             track is declared to be. Measured before this line changed, on
             case-crossover and self-controlled-case-series — the two builders
             that ship this editor today — the row's intrinsic width was a fixed
             784px at every viewport, so at 1024px, 1280px and 1440px the Reason
             box and the ✕ were painted PAST the panel's right edge and under
             the preview column, which comes later in DOM order and covers them.
             The user could fill four of HARPER's five columns and not reach the
             fifth, and could not delete a row at all; between 640px and 768px
             the whole page grew a horizontal scrollbar. Nothing errored, and a
             page-level scrollWidth assertion at 390px and 1024px — which is
             what two earlier runs measured — cannot see any of it, because at
             1024px the overflow lands inside the viewport, on top of the
             preview, rather than past its edge.

             The form column is 422-550px on every desktop viewport this site
             has a layout for, so the six-track row is in practice reached only
             between roughly 700px and 900px, where the page is one column. The
             STACKED state is therefore the normal one, and it has to stand on
             its own — which is why each box carries a visible caption below,
             rather than relying on a placeholder that disappears the moment
             anything is typed into it. */
          row.className = "grid gap-1.5 @[36rem]:gap-1.5 @[36rem]:grid-cols-[minmax(0,8rem)_minmax(0,6rem)_minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1.4fr)_minmax(0,1.4fr)_auto] items-start rounded border border-slate-200 p-2 @[36rem]:border-0 @[36rem]:p-0";
          // Stacked, five boxes in a column are indistinguishable once they are
          // filled, so say which amendment they are. Hidden where the row has
          // its own columns; the aria-labels carry the same thing either way.
          var hdr = document.createElement("p");
          hdr.className = "@[36rem]:hidden text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-600";
          hdr.textContent = "Amendment " + (i + 1);
          row.appendChild(hdr);
          AM_COLS.forEach(function (label, j) {
            // The caption is the placeholder's replacement, not its companion:
            // a placeholder is gone as soon as the box is filled, which is
            // exactly when a reader needs to know which column they are looking
            // at. sr-only rather than hidden in the wide state, so the column
            // heading a screen reader announces does not depend on the width.
            var wrap = document.createElement("label");
            wrap.className = "grid gap-0.5 min-w-0";
            var cap = document.createElement("span");
            cap.className = "@[36rem]:sr-only text-[10px] font-medium text-slate-500";
            cap.textContent = label;
            cap.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
            wrap.appendChild(cap);
            var inp = document.createElement("input");
            inp.className = INPUT;
            inp.placeholder = label;
            inp.setAttribute("aria-label", label + ", amendment " + (i + 1));
            var raw = r[j] == null ? "" : String(r[j]);
            if (j === 0) {
              /* An <input type="date"> runs the HTML value-sanitisation
                 algorithm: a value that is not a valid yyyy-mm-dd string is
                 discarded outright and the control reads back "". Nothing here
                 ever copies the DOM back into `rows` — writeRows serialises the
                 array, and only the edited cell is copied back by the listener
                 below — so the original string SURVIVES and is exported. A
                 saved draft or a ?seed= link carrying "04/03/2026" therefore
                 showed an EMPTY date box on screen while the Word file printed
                 04/03/2026, and a row sitting between two dated rows read as
                 one the sender had forgotten to date. Nothing was lost and
                 nothing errored; the screen and the document simply disagreed.
                 So show the value in a control that can hold it and mark it,
                 rather than leaving the user to infer a date is missing from a
                 document that has one. Typing a real date here restores the
                 picker on the next repaint. */
              if (dateShowable(raw)) {
                inp.type = "date";
                inp.value = raw;
              } else {
                inp.type = "text";
                inp.value = raw;
                inp.className = INPUT + " border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 text-amber-900";
                inp.title = "Not a calendar date, so the date picker cannot show it. It is exported exactly as written.";
              }
            } else {
              inp.type = "text";
              inp.value = raw;
            }
            inp.addEventListener("input", function () {
              // One keystroke into a box the builder blanked behind the panel
              // used to write the whole stale model back. The keystroke is lost
              // and the boxes repaint from the field, which is the only list
              // that exports.
              if (stale()) { sync(); return; }
              r[j] = inp.value; writeRows(rows); paintNotes(rows);
            });
            wrap.appendChild(inp);
            row.appendChild(wrap);
          });
          var del = document.createElement("button");
          del.type = "button";
          /* `justify-self-end` and a border, because stacked this button is a
             grid item in a one-column grid: without them it was a 550px-wide
             borderless strip carrying one glyph, sitting eight pixels above the
             next amendment's Version-date box — a full-width click target that
             deletes an entire amendment with no undo, painted where the eye
             reads a separator. It also says what it removes when there are no
             column headings beside it to imply it. */
          del.className = "justify-self-end text-xs text-slate-500 hover:text-rose-600 border border-slate-200 hover:border-rose-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 transition";
          del.innerHTML = '<span class="@[36rem]:hidden">Remove this amendment </span><span aria-hidden="true">✕</span>';
          del.setAttribute("aria-label", "Remove amendment " + (i + 1));
          del.addEventListener("click", function () {
            if (stale()) { sync(); return; }
            rows.splice(i, 1); writeRows(rows); paint(rows);
          });
          row.appendChild(del);
          rowsEl.appendChild(row);
        });
        // Say what the EXPORT will say, not what the screen happens to hold.
        paintNotes(rows);
      };
      /* The one place the model is rebuilt from the field — a builder that
         resets or restores writes the field directly, and this is how the
         editor catches up with it. */
      /* This is also the only place that can see a refused entry ARRIVE — from
         a ?seed= link, a restored draft, a paste, or a builder's own reset — so
         it is where the panel opens itself. The badge alone would say something
         is wrong and still leave the line that is wrong, and the ✕ that removes
         it, behind a click on a collapsed section. Typing never runs sync, so a
         panel the user closes stays closed. */
      var sync = function () {
        model = readRows();
        lastWritten = field.value;
        paint(model);
        if (model.some(isRaw)) box.open = true;
      };

      /* The new row starts EMPTY. It used to be prefilled with today's date,
         which quietly defeated the invariant writeRows states in its own
         comment three lines up: a row carrying a date is not blank, so the
         filter kept it, and one exploratory click on "+ Add an amendment" —
         exactly what a first-time visitor does to see what the panel is for —
         put "The protocol has been amended 1 time" and a row of four em dashes
         into the exported protocol. A reviewer reads that as an undocumented
         protocol change. Today's date is one field's worth of typing; an
         amendment nobody made is not recoverable once the file is sent. */
      box.querySelector("[data-pc-am-add]").addEventListener("click", function () {
        // Adding a row is still the right thing to do after the field moved
        // underneath the panel — it just has to be added to what the field
        // holds now, not to what the panel was last showing.
        if (stale()) sync();
        model.push(["", "", "", "", ""]);
        writeRows(model); paint(model);
      });
      // A builder that resets or restores the form writes the hidden field
      // directly. Rebuild if that happened behind the editor's back, so the
      // rows can never disagree with the field the export reads.
      form.addEventListener("change", function () { if (field.value !== lastWritten) sync(); });
      box.__pcSync = sync;
      sync();
      return box;
    }
    function syncAmendments(form) {
      var b = form && form.querySelector("[data-pc-amendments]");
      if (b && b.__pcSync) b.__pcSync();
    }

    // ── Markdown ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    function abstractMd(s) {
      // The amendments log rides with the abstract because HARPER puts it in
      // front matter (item 3, right after item 2) and because every builder
      // already calls this one function — so the item is satisfied everywhere
      // without nine separate edits to nine export functions.
      return "## Structured abstract\n" + abstractLines(s).map((l) => "**" + l[0].trim() + "** " + l[1]).join(" ") + "\n\n"
        + amendmentsMd(s) + "\n";
    }
    function tailMd(s) {
      return tailSections(s).map(([key, h, lines, kind]) =>
        "## " + h + "\n" + (kind === "bullets" ? lines.map((x) => "- " + x).join("\n") : lines.join("\n"))
      ).join("\n\n");
    }

    // ── docx (needs the docx global D) ────────────────────────
    function docxKit(D) {
      const FONT = "Times New Roman";
      const text = (str, o = {}) => new D.TextRun({ text: String(str == null ? "" : str), font: FONT, size: 22, ...o });
      const p = (ch, o = {}) => new D.Paragraph({ children: Array.isArray(ch) ? ch : [ch], spacing: { before: 60, after: 60, ...(o.spacing || {}) }, ...o });
      const h1 = (str) => new D.Paragraph({ children: [text(str, { bold: true, size: 28 })], spacing: { before: 200, after: 80 }, heading: D.HeadingLevel.HEADING_1 });
      const bullet = (str) => new D.Paragraph({ children: [text(str)], bullet: { level: 0 }, spacing: { before: 0, after: 0 } });
      const border = { style: D.BorderStyle.SINGLE, size: 4, color: "BBBBBB" };
      const cb = { top: border, bottom: border, left: border, right: border };
      const cell = (str, w, opt) => new D.TableCell(Object.assign({
        borders: cb, width: { size: w, type: D.WidthType.DXA },
        margins: { top: 60, bottom: 60, left: 100, right: 100 },
        children: [p([text(str)])],
      }, opt || {}));
      const headCell = (str, w) => new D.TableCell({
        borders: cb, width: { size: w, type: D.WidthType.DXA },
        shading: { fill: "F5F5F5", type: D.ShadingType.CLEAR },
        margins: { top: 60, bottom: 60, left: 100, right: 100 },
        children: [p([text(str, { bold: true })])],
      });
      return { text, p, h1, bullet, cell, headCell };
    }
    function amendmentsDocx(D, s) {
      const k = docxKit(D);
      const log = amLog(s);
      const out = [k.h1("Amendments and updates"), k.p([k.text(amendmentIntro(s, log))])];
      if (!log.length) return out;
      const W = [560, 1180, 1180, 1560, 2400, 2146];
      // Same one-pass rule and the same numbering as amendmentsMd, so the Word
      // file and the Markdown cannot disagree about which entry is which.
      if (log.some((e) => e.ok)) {
        const header = new D.TableRow({ tableHeader: true, children: ["No."].concat(AM_COLS).map((c, i) => k.headCell(c, W[i])) });
        /* The refusal notice spans the five data columns rather than sitting in
           the first of them. Confirmed in a real word/document.xml: it was
           written into the cell under "Version date", 1180 DXA ≈ 0.82 inch
           wide, with the other four empty — an 88-character sentence wrapping
           down a date column, which is exactly the shape a reader skims past as
           a mangled date. A merged cell also matches what the stub is for: it
           asserts nothing about any one column, because the tool does not know
           which value belongs in which. The Markdown table keeps five cells,
           since GFM has no way to merge them; both carry the same sentence. */
        const stubW = W.slice(1).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
        const body = log.map((e) => new D.TableRow({
          children: e.ok
            ? [String(e.no)].concat(e.cells.map((c) => c || "—")).map((c, j) => k.cell(c, W[j]))
            : [k.cell(String(e.no), W[0]), k.cell(amStubNotice(e), stubW, { columnSpan: AM_COLS.length })],
        }));
        out.push(new D.Table({ width: { size: 9026, type: D.WidthType.DXA }, columnWidths: W, rows: [header].concat(body) }));
      }
      const bad = log.filter((e) => !e.ok);
      if (bad.length) {
        out.push(k.p([k.text(AM_REFUSE_HEAD, { bold: true })]));
        out.push(k.p([k.text(AM_REFUSE_LEAD)]));
        bad.forEach((e) => {
          out.push(k.p([k.text(amRefuseLabel(e), { bold: true })]));
          // Monospaced, so a reader can see the delimiters they need to fix.
          out.push(k.p([k.text(e.line, { font: "Courier New" })]));
        });
      }
      return out;
    }
    function abstractDocx(D, s) {
      const k = docxKit(D);
      const out = [k.h1("Structured abstract")];
      abstractLines(s).forEach((l) => out.push(k.p([k.text(l[0], { bold: true }), k.text(l[1])])));
      amendmentsDocx(D, s).forEach((c) => out.push(c));
      return out;
    }
    function tailDocx(D, s) {
      const k = docxKit(D), out = [];
      tailSections(s).forEach(([key, h, lines, kind]) => {
        out.push(k.h1(h));
        if (kind === "bullets") lines.forEach((b) => out.push(k.bullet(b)));
        else lines.forEach((b) => out.push(k.p([k.text(b)])));
      });
      return out;
    }

    /* ── Seeding a builder from a link ───────────────────────────────────
       A study proposal that already exists elsewhere — in the Hot Topic
       platform, in an email, in a colleague's notes — should not have to be
       retyped into these forms field by field. `?seed=` carries a state object
       in the same shape the builder already saves to localStorage.

       base64url, so the value survives being pasted into chat clients and
       mail readers that mangle raw JSON in a query string.

       Returns null when there is no seed, so callers keep their existing
       localStorage restore as the fallback and behave exactly as before. */
    function readSeed() {
      try {
        var raw = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("seed");
        if (!raw) return null;
        var b64 = raw.replace(/-/g, "+").replace(/_/g, "/");
        while (b64.length % 4) b64 += "=";
        var json = decodeURIComponent(escape(atob(b64)));  // atob is byte-wise; undo UTF-8
        var obj = JSON.parse(json);
        return obj && typeof obj === "object" ? obj : null;
      } catch (e) {
        console.warn("[seed] ignoring unreadable seed:", e);
        return null;
      }
    }

    /* Write a flat {fieldName: value} object into a form. Mirrors what the
       builders' own writeForm does, for the pages that restore inline. */
    function applySeed(form, state) {
      if (!form || !state) return false;
      for (var k in state) {
        var els = form.elements[k];
        if (!els) continue;
        var val = state[k];

        // form.elements[name] is a single element only when the name is unique.
        // Share a name across several controls — every checkbox group here does
        // — and it is a RadioNodeList, which has no .type, so the old code fell
        // through to `els.value = val`. On a RadioNodeList that setter only
        // ticks a matching RADIO; for a checkbox group it is a silent no-op, so
        // a seeded set of ticks was dropped without a word.
        var many = els.tagName === undefined && typeof els.length === "number";
        var nodes = many ? Array.prototype.slice.call(els) : [els];
        var first = nodes[0];
        if (!first) continue;

        if (first.type === "checkbox") {
          if (Array.isArray(val)) {
            var want = val.map(String);
            nodes.forEach(function (n) { n.checked = want.indexOf(n.value) >= 0; });
          } else if (typeof val === "boolean" || val == null) {
            nodes.forEach(function (n) { n.checked = !!val; });
          } else {
            // A lone checkbox stored as its own value, or "true"/"1".
            var sv = String(val);
            nodes.forEach(function (n) { n.checked = n.value === sv || sv === "true" || sv === "1" || sv === "on"; });
          }
        } else if (first.type === "radio") {
          // Assigning .value here would rewrite the option a radio stands for.
          var rv = Array.isArray(val) ? String(val[0]) : String(val == null ? "" : val);
          nodes.forEach(function (n) { n.checked = n.value === rv; });
        } else if (Array.isArray(val)) {
          nodes.forEach(function (n, i) { if (val[i] != null) n.value = val[i]; });
        } else {
          nodes.forEach(function (n) { n.value = val == null ? "" : val; });
        }
      }
      syncAmendments(form);
      return true;
    }

    /* `?seed=paste` — the payload was too large to travel in the URL.
       A worked-up proposal carries 600-900 characters per operational
       definition, which base64-encodes past 20,000 characters and is refused
       as "URI Too Long", so the sender copies it and this offers somewhere to
       put it. Keeping it off the query string also keeps an unpublished study
       out of browser history. */
    function pastePanel(form, write, storageKey) {
      if (document.getElementById("pc-paste")) return;

      var applyText = function (raw, onFail) {
        raw = (raw || "").trim();
        if (!raw) { onFail && onFail("剪貼簿是空的"); return false; }
        var obj;
        try { obj = JSON.parse(raw); } catch (e) {
          onFail && onFail("看不懂這段內容 — 請確認是從 Hot Topic 的「開啟 Protocol」複製的");
          return false;
        }
        /* This is the moment the draft is really replaced, so this is where it
           is set aside — once, when something is actually pasted, rather than
           on every arrival where a repeat visit would overwrite the copy with
           the previous visit's leftovers. */
        var backup = null;
        if (storageKey) {
          try { backup = localStorage.getItem(storageKey); } catch (e) { console.warn("[PC] could not read the saved draft", e); }
          if (backup) {
            try { localStorage.setItem(storageKey + ".before-seed", backup); }
            catch (e) { console.warn("[PC] could not set the draft aside", e); backup = null; }
          }
        }
        /* Reset first, so a pasted protocol REPLACES the design rather than
           merging into whatever the form was holding. The form now carries the
           user's own draft (restore() loads it on this path), and write() only
           assigns the keys the payload actually carries — so without this the
           pasted study inherited the reader's population, comparator and
           sampling scheme, and the document that came out named two different
           studies at once. `form.reset()` restores the markup defaults, which
           is exactly where a ?seed= link lands, so the two hand-off routes
           agree about what an unmentioned field means. */
        try { form.reset(); } catch (e) { console.warn("[PC] could not reset the form", e); }
        write(obj);
        var b = document.getElementById("pc-paste");
        if (b) b.remove();
        banner(backup ? storageKey : null,
          "This form has been filled in from the pasted protocol; fields it did not mention are back at this page's defaults."
          + (backup ? " Your previous draft has been set aside — use the button below to put it back. 此表單已由貼上的內容填入,你原本的草稿已另存備份。" : " 此表單已由貼上的內容填入。"));
        form.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", { bubbles: true }));
        return true;
      };

      /* Try to read the clipboard outright. A browser will not let a page do
         this silently the first time — it prompts, and the prompt needs the
         document focused — but once the user allows it for this site, every
         later hand-off fills the form with no interaction at all. The panel
         below only appears when that fails. */
      var auto = function () {
        if (!navigator.clipboard || !navigator.clipboard.readText) return Promise.resolve(false);
        return navigator.clipboard.readText().then(
          function (txt) { return applyText(txt); },
          function () { return false; }
        );
      };

      auto().then(function (done) {
        if (done) return;

        var box = document.createElement("div");
        box.id = "pc-paste";
        box.style.cssText =
          "margin:0 0 1rem;padding:.9rem 1rem;border-radius:10px;" +
          "background:#EAF3EE;border:1px solid #9CC9AE;";
        box.innerHTML =
          '<div style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.35rem">從 Hot Topic 帶入研究設計</div>' +
          '<div style="font-size:.85rem;color:#3D5560;margin-bottom:.6rem">' +
          // "你目前的草稿在套用前不會被改動" — your current draft will not be
          // modified before you apply — was false when this panel first shipped:
          // arriving here left the form at its defaults and the caller's first
          // autosave wrote those over the draft. restore() now loads the draft
          // on this path, so the sentence is true, and the clause after it says
          // what happens when you DO apply.
          '內容已複製到剪貼簿。按下面的按鈕貼上 — 瀏覽器會問一次是否允許讀取剪貼簿,' +
          '允許之後以後就會自動填入。你目前的草稿在套用前不會被改動;套用時會另存備份,可以取回。</div>' +
          '<div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap">' +
          '<button type="button" id="pc-paste-read" style="font:inherit;cursor:pointer;' +
          'background:#3F8C6E;color:#fff;border:0;border-radius:6px;padding:.4rem 1rem">' +
          '從剪貼簿貼上</button>' +
          '<button type="button" id="pc-paste-manual" style="font:inherit;cursor:pointer;' +
          'background:transparent;color:#3D5560;border:1px solid #9CC9AE;border-radius:6px;' +
          'padding:.4rem .9rem">改用手動貼上</button>' +
          '<span id="pc-paste-msg" style="font-size:.85rem;color:#9E3A2C"></span></div>' +
          '<textarea id="pc-paste-in" rows="3" style="display:none;width:100%;margin-top:.6rem;' +
          'font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:.8rem;padding:.5rem;' +
          'border:1px solid #9CC9AE;border-radius:6px"></textarea>';
        var f = document.querySelector("form");
        if (f && f.parentNode) f.parentNode.insertBefore(box, f);

        var msg = box.querySelector("#pc-paste-msg");
        var ta = box.querySelector("#pc-paste-in");
        var fail = function (m) { msg.textContent = m; };

        box.querySelector("#pc-paste-read").addEventListener("click", function () {
          msg.textContent = "";
          if (!navigator.clipboard || !navigator.clipboard.readText) {
            ta.style.display = "block"; ta.focus();
            fail("這個瀏覽器不支援讀取剪貼簿,請手動貼上");
            return;
          }
          navigator.clipboard.readText().then(
            function (txt) { applyText(txt, fail); },
            function () {
              ta.style.display = "block"; ta.focus();
              fail("瀏覽器不允許讀取剪貼簿,請在下方手動貼上 (Ctrl+V)");
            }
          );
        });

        box.querySelector("#pc-paste-manual").addEventListener("click", function () {
          ta.style.display = "block"; ta.focus(); msg.textContent = "";
        });
        ta.addEventListener("paste", function () {
          setTimeout(function () { applyText(ta.value, fail); }, 0);
        });
      });
    }

    /* One call for the common case: seed if present, else restore the saved
       draft. Returns "seed" | "paste" | "saved" | "none" so a page can tell
       the user. */
    function restore(form, storageKey, writeForm) {
      if (form) ensureAmendmentField(form);   // must exist before anything writes into it
      var write = function (s) {
        (writeForm || function (x) { applySeed(form, x); })(s);
        syncAmendments(form);
      };
      if (new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("seed") === "paste") {
        /* This returned without setting the draft aside, and that lost it.
           The paste path deliberately does NOT load the saved draft — the
           pasted proposal is meant to land on the page's own defaults, the
           same as ?seed= — but the caller's first render() then autosaves that
           default form straight over the draft key, within milliseconds and
           before the user has pasted anything or pressed a thing. Measured on
           the four builders that have no second restore loop of their own
           (ACNU, SCCS, case-crossover, descriptive-analysis): type a title,
           let it save, open ?seed=paste, paste nothing — the title is gone
           from the form and from storage, with no backup key anywhere.

           The panel this path mounts said, on screen, at that moment:
           "你目前的草稿在套用前不會被改動" — your current draft will not be
           modified before you apply. It had already been destroyed.

           Taking a `.before-seed` copy on ARRIVAL is not the fix, and the first
           attempt at it here was worse than the bug: visit the link a second
           time and the copy is overwritten by the wreckage the first visit
           left, so the recovery button restores an empty form while promising
           otherwise. Backing up on arrival cannot work, because nothing
           distinguishes a real draft from the defaults a previous visit saved.

           So don't create the wreckage. Load the draft, and the caller's
           autosave writes back the same draft it read — nothing is displaced,
           nothing needs setting aside, and the panel's original promise that
           the draft is untouched until you apply becomes true instead of being
           reworded. The copy is taken in pastePanel at the moment something is
           actually pasted, which is the moment the draft is really replaced. */
        var pre = null;
        try { pre = localStorage.getItem(storageKey); } catch (e) { console.warn("[PC] could not read the saved draft", e); }
        if (pre) {
          try { write(JSON.parse(pre)); }
          catch (e) { console.warn("[PC] saved draft could not be read", e); }
        }
        pastePanel(form, write, storageKey);
        return "paste";
      }
      var seeded = readSeed();
      if (seeded) {
        /* Copy the saved draft aside BEFORE the seed lands on top of it.
           Without this the banner below was simply untrue: a builder's first
           render() autosaves the form to the very same storage key, and that
           happens within a few milliseconds of the seed being applied — before
           the document has even finished parsing. So by the time the user read
           "your previous draft is still in the browser, reload without the seed
           parameter to get it back" and did exactly that, what came back was
           the seed. One key, no copy, nothing typed surviving: a draft built
           over an afternoon could be destroyed by opening a colleague's link,
           with the page asserting the opposite on screen. */
        var backup = null;
        try { backup = localStorage.getItem(storageKey); } catch (e) { console.warn("[PC] could not read the saved draft", e); }
        if (backup) {
          try { localStorage.setItem(storageKey + ".before-seed", backup); }
          catch (e) { console.warn("[PC] could not set the draft aside", e); backup = null; }
        }
        write(seeded);
        banner(backup ? storageKey : null);
        return "seed";
      }
      try {
        var saved = localStorage.getItem(storageKey);
        if (saved) { write(JSON.parse(saved)); return "saved"; }
      } catch (e) {
        console.warn("[PC] saved draft could not be read", e);
      }
      return "none";
    }

    /* Say where the values came from, and — when there was a draft to lose —
       give back the only thing that can actually recover it. The previous
       version told the user in Chinese, on pages written in English, to do
       something that did not work (see restore() above). It now states what
       happened and offers a button that puts the draft back and reloads
       without the seed. `storageKey` is null when there was no draft to
       displace, in which case there is nothing to offer. */
    function banner(storageKey, lead) {
      if (document.getElementById("pc-seed-note")) return;
      var d = document.createElement("div");
      d.id = "pc-seed-note";
      d.setAttribute("role", "status");
      d.style.cssText =
        "margin:0 0 1rem;padding:.6rem .85rem;border-radius:8px;font-size:.85rem;" +
        "background:#E8F3EC;border:1px solid #9CC9AE;color:#1E5B3A;";

      var p = document.createElement("p");
      p.style.cssText = "margin:0";
      // The paste path needs its own lead: nothing has been pre-filled there
      // yet — the form is showing the page's defaults and the user has not
      // pasted anything — so the sentence below would be false on it.
      p.textContent = lead || (storageKey
        ? "This form was pre-filled from a link. Your previous draft has been set aside — it is NOT recoverable by reloading, because the link's values are saved over it. 此表單由連結預先填入,你原本的草稿已另存備份。"
        : "This form was pre-filled from a link. 此表單由連結預先填入。");
      d.appendChild(p);

      if (storageKey) {
        var b = document.createElement("button");
        b.type = "button";
        b.textContent = "Restore my previous draft / 取回我原本的草稿";
        b.style.cssText =
          "margin-top:.5rem;font:inherit;cursor:pointer;background:#3F8C6E;color:#fff;" +
          "border:0;border-radius:6px;padding:.35rem .9rem";
        b.addEventListener("click", function () {
          try {
            var saved = localStorage.getItem(storageKey + ".before-seed");
            if (saved == null) { b.textContent = "The backup could not be read."; return; }
            localStorage.setItem(storageKey, saved);
          } catch (e) {
            console.warn("[PC] could not restore the draft", e);
            b.textContent = "This browser would not let the draft be restored.";
            return;
          }
          // Reload WITHOUT the seed, so the restored draft is what loads.
          location.replace(location.pathname + location.hash);
        });
        d.appendChild(b);
      }

      var f = document.querySelector("form");
      if (f && f.parentNode) f.parentNode.insertBefore(d, f);
    }

    window.PC = { abstractMd, tailMd, abstractDocx, tailDocx, mdCell: mdCell,
                  readSeed: readSeed, applySeed: applySeed, restore: restore,
                  mountAmendments: mountAmendments, syncAmendments: syncAmendments,
                  amendmentRows: amendmentRows, amendmentsMd: amendmentsMd, amendmentsDocx: amendmentsDocx };
  })();
</script>
